mestamp": "2026-04-25T16:10:12Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "11.\tX was a professor of astronomy in Uppsala and made several important contributions to science — but he is remembered today for just one invention, a system used by scientists everywhere (and the rest of us in most of the world). The original system that X designed was “upside down” from our perspective: he assigned zero to what we consider very high and 100 to what we consider very low. His colleague, the botanist Carl Linnaeus of taxonomy fame, inverted it to the form we use today after X’s death. X died of tuberculosis on April 25, 1744, at just 42 years old. For most of the following two centuries, his system was known not by his name but by a term that means “hundred steps.” In 1948 the scientific community formally renamed it after him, which was an easy change because most scientific notation stayed the same anyway. ID X (10 points). ‎<attached: 00126846-PHOTO-2026-04-25-09-10-12.jpg>", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T16:10:22Z", "username": "Akshay", "text": "Fahrenheit", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T16:10:24Z", "username": "Abhishek Vats", "text": "Celsius", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T16:10:25Z", "username": "Akshay", "text": "Temperature", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T16:10:29Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Celsius", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T16:10:30Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "Celsius", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-25T16:10:36Z", "username": "Saumay", "text": "Celsius", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"times